WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.580
1 million orbital computers, 1 US approval, moving heavy data work off Earth. This is Omni View,

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:14.020
episode 249. After that, New Zealand's unfinished government, a German church liability ruling,

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:20.700
and Costa Rica turning away a United Nations rapporteur, then European prices, growth in Gaza's

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:27.260
children, and retired buses put back to work. The Federal Communications Commission, the US agency

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:32.960
that licenses radio uses, including satellites, has told SpaceX it may put a plan called

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.380
Star Mind into orbit.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.840
Al Jazeera reports that Chairman Brendan Carr described the grant as a first in the country's

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:40.200
history.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:46.160
His wording was that SpaceX may deploy 1 million orbital compute modules to process massive

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.180
amounts of data for use back on Earth.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:53.380
He tied the move to President Donald Trump and said the aim is opportunity, investment,

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.280
and benefits for the American people.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:01.460
SpaceX says each satellite would run artificial intelligence, software that finds patterns in huge

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:07.460
data sets in a low latency sun synchronous orbit and send the results down, drawing power from

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.820
the sun. The company calls that the fastest way to grow because electrical power on the ground

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:18.740
is getting harder to find and it calls the setup low cost and high performance. The license sets

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:25.920
a clock. Work must start no later than November 2033. 100,000 modules, a tenth of the maximum,

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:33.120
must be up no later than November 2035. Half by November 2038. The whole constellation by

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:40.800
2040. Sad Fleet Live, which tracks satellites in real time, counts 16,928 active satellites

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:49.320
now. 11,190 of them, 66% are already SpaceX Starlink internet satellites, so this would stack

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.940
computing on top of a fleet that is already most of what is up there. Ground data centers need land,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:02:01.260
water, and power, and they throw off heat. An article a US nonprofit, the Environmental and Energy

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:07.320
Study Institute, published in 2025, said a large data center can use up to 5 million gallons of

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.280
water a day, roughly a town of 10,000 to 50,000 people.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.040
Those pressures have drawn protests in the United States, Kenya and India.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:23.020
In July, opponents of rapid data center growth held 142 protests across 42 U.S. states.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:28.240
Al Jazeera says polling data showed that opposition was a key issue for many voters in this

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:29.400
month's midterm elections.

00:02:30.575 --> 00:02:35.775
New Zealand's final election count, released Friday from the November 7th vote, leaves Prime

00:02:35.775 --> 00:02:41.335
Minister Christopher Luxon won seat short of a majority. Al Jazeera reports that his National

00:02:41.335 --> 00:02:48.375
Party plus populist New Zealand First and Libertarian Act won 65 seats. National 40, Act

00:02:48.375 --> 00:02:56.395
13, New Zealand First 12. Parliament has 130 members, so 66 are required. Labour, the largest

00:02:56.395 --> 00:03:03.755
opposition party won 36. Qilai Wong's new centrist opportunity party won 7. The Associated Press

00:03:03.755 --> 00:03:09.755
reads the same deadlock the same way. The centrist newcomer is the decider. Al Jazeera ties the

00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:15.535
shortfall to lost support in a stagnant economy. Luxon has been prime minister since 2023.

00:03:16.255 --> 00:03:21.915
If nobody agrees, voters could be back at the polls next year. Luke Oldfield, a political scientist

00:03:21.915 --> 00:03:27.395
at Victoria University of Wellington, told Reuters a deal was likely, and that a right-hand

00:03:27.395 --> 00:03:32.995
block was highly likely rather than a second election. Both camps have to live with 65 seats

00:03:32.995 --> 00:03:37.415
against a 66-seat bar, and Wong's 7 are the only ready bridge.

00:03:37.935 --> 00:03:43.075
Luxon told a news conference that New Zealanders need a government that is stable, works together,

00:03:43.315 --> 00:03:44.435
and lasts the full term.

00:03:45.035 --> 00:03:48.935
That is the argument for adding one partner instead of sending people back to vote.

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:54.735
Wong has promised a universal basic income of up to $370 New Zealand dollars a week,

00:03:55.095 --> 00:04:01.675
about $208 US dollars, plus more climate action, a ban on bottom trawling, healthcare reforms,

00:04:01.675 --> 00:04:03.435
and a kinder style of politics.

00:04:03.935 --> 00:04:07.335
She has also pledged to do everything she can for a stable government.

00:04:07.795 --> 00:04:09.555
That package is what her seats cost.

00:04:10.235 --> 00:04:12.615
It runs into partners Luxon already has.

00:04:13.055 --> 00:04:17.455
Winston Peters of New Zealand First wants tighter immigration and withdrawal from the Paris

00:04:17.455 --> 00:04:20.055
Climate Accord, the global climate treaty.

00:04:20.535 --> 00:04:25.255
He has called opponents Morons and told a Chinese-born lawmaker to go back to your country.

00:04:25.875 --> 00:04:30.435
Deputy Prime Minister David Seymour of Act campaigned against a greater governance role

00:04:30.435 --> 00:04:34.875
for the indigenous Maori minority, and promise large cuts to state spending.

00:04:35.635 --> 00:04:40.875
Wong's stability pledge does not erase that clash, and Luxon's case for a lasting government

00:04:40.875 --> 00:04:43.135
still needs a partner those two may refuse.

00:04:43.935 --> 00:04:47.315
Oldfield's reading is that a right-hand deal is still the likely end.

00:04:47.855 --> 00:04:52.815
Watch whether Luxon names a governing partner, or whether a fresh election next year becomes

00:04:52.815 --> 00:04:53.275
the path.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.740
A German appeals court has put a new price on church liability for a priest's abuse.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.020
Doichavella reports that on Thursday, the higher regional court of Cologne ordered the

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:12.880
archdiocese of Cologne to pay 550,000 euros, about $603,341, to a woman now 60.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.920
Melanie F. was subjected to serious sexual abuse by a priest in the late 1970s and early

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:19.860
1980s.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:25.800
She had already received 360,000 euros, so the court added 190,000.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:31.700
The previous High Court Award in a similar case was 300,000 euros from the regional court

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:33.620
of Cologne in June 2023.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.720
The seventh civil senate pointed to an abortion performed without her knowledge or consent

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.480
when she was 15 as a reason to raise the damages.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.960
She reportedly became pregnant twice during the abuse.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:49.480
Born in 1966, she lived in a Catholic orphanage in Bonn.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:55.780
Burnt you, then a theology student and volunteer, later in ordained priest, took her and

00:05:55.780 --> 00:06:01.420
boy two years older to live with him. From 1979 he abused her in his residence at the

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:06.560
parish and threatened to send her back to the orphanage. He became legal guardian of both children.

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:11.660
Cardinal Joseph Huffner, then Archbishop of Cologne, approved that arrangement.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:18.060
The higher court held the Archdiocese liable under official liability. It overruled the 2023

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:23.100
regional ruling, which had said the abuse was not sufficiently tied to his role as a priest

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.860
least, and had happened in his capacity as a caregiver.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:30.840
The higher court said he gained her trust in a pastoral role the judges called a cover,

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:35.440
that he would likely not have become foster father so easily without the cover of a

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:39.140
celibate priest, and that most of the abuse took place in the rectory.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:45.300
Deutscheweller reports that the 2023 ruling met disbelief inside an archdiocese already

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.460
in turmoil over how Cardinal Rainer Maria Wolke handled abuse cases.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:53.900
after decades of church teaching that a priest is a priest in everything he does.

00:06:54.905 --> 00:06:58.685
Costa Rica has stopped a United Nations special rapporteur at the airport.

00:06:59.525 --> 00:07:05.165
Al Jazeera reports that Francesca Albany's, the rapporteur on the occupied Palestinian territories,

00:07:05.745 --> 00:07:09.825
was halted and held on Thursday at Juan Santa Maria International Airport.

00:07:10.405 --> 00:07:14.805
She is an independent expert named by the UN Human Rights Council in 2022

00:07:14.805 --> 00:07:17.485
and she does not speak for the UN itself.

00:07:18.045 --> 00:07:23.485
Ulmer Badilla, Director-General of the Immigration Authority, said she was refused entry because

00:07:23.485 --> 00:07:28.365
she is the subject of an international alert over links to terrorist acts, and that police

00:07:28.365 --> 00:07:31.085
enforce the law when national security is at risk.

00:07:31.645 --> 00:07:33.825
He did not name the country that issued the alert.

00:07:34.305 --> 00:07:38.365
She was traveling to receive an honorary doctorate from the University of Costa Rica.

00:07:38.925 --> 00:07:43.645
She has faced pushback from Israel and the United States over her advocacy for Palestinians

00:07:43.645 --> 00:07:47.725
and her accusations that Israel has committed genocide and other war crimes.

00:07:48.445 --> 00:07:53.085
The United States imposed sanctions on her after she criticized Washington's policy on Gaza.

00:07:53.905 --> 00:07:59.145
President Laura Fernandez, in office since May, is a close ally of President Trump and has

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:05.425
pursued closer trade ties with Israel. Israel declared Albanese persona non-grata in 2024.

00:08:06.305 --> 00:08:10.705
Badilla's account is that an international alert tying her to terrorist acts made entry

00:08:10.705 --> 00:08:15.365
a national security refusal, and that Costa Rican police had to apply that rule.

00:08:16.065 --> 00:08:21.725
The strongest case for going further, as Israel's ambassador to the UN, Danny Danon, put

00:08:21.725 --> 00:08:26.885
it while praising Costa Rica, is that someone who uses a UN mandate to spread propaganda,

00:08:27.465 --> 00:08:32.405
legitimize terrorist organizations, desecrate the memory of the six million Jews murdered

00:08:32.405 --> 00:08:36.985
in the Holocaust, and vilify the United States and Israel should face consequences.

00:08:37.845 --> 00:08:40.425
Albinese called the detention a disgrace and appalling.

00:08:40.865 --> 00:08:44.205
She wrote that you see the face of the empire the day you touch a nerve.

00:08:44.765 --> 00:08:50.085
She told the student outlet, Seminario University Dad, that being on the US sanctions list

00:08:50.085 --> 00:08:52.105
did not explain a stop in Costa Rica.

00:08:52.725 --> 00:08:54.605
We are not in the United States, she said.

00:08:55.065 --> 00:08:56.045
We are in Costa Rica.

00:08:56.665 --> 00:08:58.265
Is Costa Rica a sovereign country?

00:08:58.865 --> 00:09:01.945
She said the only country she knew she could not enter was Israel.

00:09:01.945 --> 00:09:08.165
But Deela never named the alerts author, and Albany's did not dispute that she knew she was on the U.S. list.

00:09:09.153 --> 00:09:13.393
Prices across the Eurozone, the countries that share the Euro, are climbing again.

00:09:14.193 --> 00:09:21.373
Al Jazeera reports that inflation doubled from 2% in December 2025 to 4% in October 2026,

00:09:21.833 --> 00:09:28.693
a three-year high as conflict in the Middle East disrupts oil and gas. Energy inflation hit 21% in

00:09:28.693 --> 00:09:35.693
October, the highest since December 2022, when it was 25.5% in the first winter after Russia's

00:09:35.693 --> 00:09:41.533
full-scale invasion of Ukraine. Those energy figures come from EuroStat, the European Union's

00:09:41.533 --> 00:09:46.413
Statistics Office. The European Central Bank, which sets interest rates for the Eurozone,

00:09:46.953 --> 00:09:54.273
lists Lithuania at 6.1%, Bulgaria at 5.9%, and Greece at 5.7% as the highest rates.

00:09:54.273 --> 00:10:03.093
Cypress went from 0.1% at the end of last year to 5.4. Italy went from 1.2 to 5.6. Belgium

00:10:03.093 --> 00:10:12.953
from 2.2 to 5.2. France from 0.7 to 3.7. A few eased. Slovakia fell from 4.1% to 2.9.

00:10:13.493 --> 00:10:22.433
And Algebra also lists Estonia at 3.6. Latvia at 3.1. Austria at 3.5. And Malta at 2.3.

00:10:22.433 --> 00:10:29.773
EuroStats says a typical basket of food, housing, energy and transport now costs 69% more than

00:10:29.773 --> 00:10:34.593
in the year 2000, and about two-fifths of that increase came in the past four years.

00:10:35.073 --> 00:10:38.933
In 2022, prices jump by more than 8% in a single year.

00:10:39.553 --> 00:10:44.553
Prices in Hungary have more than tripled since 2000, so something that costs 100 euros

00:10:44.553 --> 00:10:50.793
then costs about 335 euros now, after a 17% surge in 2023 alone.

00:10:50.793 --> 00:10:58.213
In Athens, Georgos, a 40-year-old journalist, said his pay rose from 800 euros to 1,100

00:10:58.213 --> 00:11:01.293
over two years, and he is still borrowing to get by.

00:11:01.933 --> 00:11:07.153
In London, Emma, 33, called nearly 10 pounds for a return to bride ridiculous.

00:11:07.913 --> 00:11:13.113
The Wall Street Journal adds a newer national reading, inflation in France and Spain fell

00:11:13.113 --> 00:11:18.353
more than expected in November as fuel prices declined, but stayed too high for the European

00:11:18.353 --> 00:11:23.233
in Central Bank, which is expected to raise its key rate for a third time since the war

00:11:23.233 --> 00:11:25.433
between the United States and Iran began.

00:11:26.412 --> 00:11:30.892
A nutrition survey in Gaza has put a number on how many young children are not growing.

00:11:31.612 --> 00:11:37.032
Al-Jazeera reports that UNICEF, the United Nations Children's Fund, and its partners surveyed

00:11:37.032 --> 00:11:42.512
accessible areas of the Gaza Strip between May 31st and June 15th, 2026.

00:11:43.192 --> 00:11:46.632
They found 12.2% of children under 5 were stunted.

00:11:46.632 --> 00:11:52.932
Only 22.4% of children aged 6 to 23 months received a minimum acceptable diet.

00:11:53.512 --> 00:11:58.792
Seven-year-old Nabeiga Al-Zagzook is 99 cm tall and weighs 10 kg.

00:11:59.392 --> 00:12:06.572
Five-year-old Tallah Zadan is 94 cm, about 15 to 16 cm shorter than the average for her age.

00:12:07.072 --> 00:12:10.132
Her father says her height has barely changed in two years.

00:12:10.132 --> 00:12:18.852
Nutritionist Nada Abu Al-Nada says average food availability was about 2,580 calories per person per day before the war,

00:12:19.232 --> 00:12:22.872
an estimated average consumption has fallen to about 1,000.

00:12:23.452 --> 00:12:29.892
At Nasser Hospital, endocrinologist Hani Sayed Zuhair says growth problem cases in a single clinic session

00:12:29.892 --> 00:12:35.112
